Dear Augustin,

(great, thanks for the data. its just way easier to play when the data are there and everything is reproduced correctly)

If I understand correctly, you want to get the information back from the plotting routine. As I understand ggplot, this is not something ggplot is typical meant to do. But there are ways, and I too think these are great to exploit.

gg=ggplot(d1m, aes(x = Sample, y = value)) + geom_boxplot()

## this was a trick offered once to me by Koshke Takahashi,
## he said it's not guranteed to work in the future.
gb <- ggplot_build(g)

## str(gb) is extensive, but look at  str(gb[[1]])
## looks like $outliers, $ymin, $ymax, and $group (though this is in order,
## so not explicitly needed) are your ticket
## extract the useful data frame
gb1=gb[[1]][[1]][[1]]   ## funny

## this is your new data frame where you'll remove the outliers
d1m.na <http://d1m.na>=d1m
## to match group you'll need to make sample a factor in the same
## default way as ggplot
d1m.na <http://d1m.na>$Sample=factor(d1m.na <http://d1m.na>$Sample)

## there maybe a cleaner way of doing this, but here goes....
if (exists('wh.na.all')) rm(wh.na.all)
## for each case / factor level in Sample
for (cc in 1:dim(gb1)[1]) {
  wh.na <http://wh.na> <- which(  as.numeric(d1m.na <http://d1m.na>$Sample)== cc &
                              (  d1m.na <http://d1m.na>$value > gb1$ymax[cc]  | d1m.na <http://d1m.na>$value < gb1$ymin[cc]) )
  if (length(wh.na <http://wh.na>)>0) {
    print(d1m.na <http://d1m.na>$value[wh.na <http://wh.na>] == gb1$outliers[[cc]]) ## just checking
    wh.na.all <- if (!exists('wh.na.all')) wh.na <http://wh.na> else c(wh.na.all,wh.na <http://wh.na>)
    d1m.na <http://d1m.na>$value[wh.na <http://wh.na>]=NA
  }
}

#just another check.
d1m$value[wh.na.all]==unlist(gb1$outliers)

I hope that achieves what you want. Let me know if not.
I'm looking forward to visiting your city (for the first time) at the end of the month!

Best,

James

